\f0\fs24 \cf0 organised for Claude as two instruction sets: current task list additions and future task records. --- **INSTRUCTION FOR CLAUDE \'97 CURRENT TASK LIST AND FUTURE RECORDS** **Context:** SageReasoning P0 Foundations. Context architecture build complete. ATRF governing document live. Q11 sequence binding. Three open questions carried: GS-ATRF-1, GS-ATRF-2, GS-ATRF-3. Drift session open: principles 4 and 5 jointly, carrying Seneca 75.8-9 criterion, M7 window question, and the discriminative range framing question added 2026-08-13. Kath\'eakon session open: principle 2. Layer 3 governance session open: S7 re-opening question. --- **CURRENT TASK LIST ADDITIONS** **Task 1 \'97 Amend the SagePals future-direction record.** File: `operations/future-directions/2026-08-13-sagepals.md` Three amendments required. Amendment 1: Add the North Star reformulation. SagePals North Star has shifted from trust-network-for-professional-collaboration to "innovation in service to the truth." The operative criterion is: SagePals rejects work that uses AI to manufacture false impressions for others. This is grounded in the synkatathesis principle applied to the output side \'97 the practitioner should not produce distorted impressions for others to assent to, by the same logic that they should not assent to distorted impressions themselves. Precision note: "innovation in service to the truth" is an aspiration and orientation, not a verification criterion. The verifiable criterion is examined assent \'97 did the practitioner examine the AI output before assenting to it and presenting it as their work? This is the task-authenticity criterion for SagePals. Amendment 2: Add the task-declaration mechanism. SagePals requires a task-declaration step where the practitioner states what AI was used for and what they brought themselves. The declaration is not verified externally. It is held against the trust record over time. The G4 cross-check analogue: the network runs both the practitioner's declaration and the observable evidence of their work, and divergence is itself a trust signal. Repeated misrepresentation shows up as a pattern in the trust record. Amendment 3: Correct and sharpen the trust-record visibility question per Claude's 2026-08-14 feedback. The question splits per surface. Agent half: already public since E1 founder election 2026-07-12; partly defended by ADR-013 \'a78 envelope and curation-via-volume disclosure; those bound the reader's inference but do not stop the subject performing \'97 that distinction must remain sharp. Human half: genuinely open and the harder problem \'97 a visible record becomes a credential, and SagePals would then risk rebuilding the credential system it exists to replace, in the vocabulary of virtue, where the corruption is harder to see. The design principle that resolves the philodoxia problem: visibility should be relational, not broadcast. Trust signals are shared within a circle with those the practitioner has already extended trust to, not displayed to strangers as credentials. This is the Seneca-to-Lucilius model, not the marketplace model. --- **Task 2 \'97 Create a new future-direction record: The Prudence Group.** File: `operations/future-directions/2026-08-14-prudence-group.md` Status: future direction \'97 documented, not scoped, not scheduled. Date written: 2026-08-14. No connection to current P0 build sequence. **What it is.** The Prudence Group is the practice layer that SagePals exists to support. It is a Stoic circle \'97 a structured philosophical examination in which one person brings a matter and the rest conduct the examination through questions only, adjudicated by a guide. It is grounded in the Epictetan diatribe form and the Socratic dialogue form, with the guide's function closer to Epictetus (directed examination from prior diagnosis) and the participant function closer to Socrates (drawing out through questions rather than asserting conclusions). **The declarations.** Opening declaration (spoken or written by the bringer at the start of every session): "There are impressions I choose to accept or reject before they arrive as thoughts that affect me and my actions. I choose with intention. I will state my matter and my role-obligations to be examined without favour by the prudence group for the diatribes to transcribe." Note: "examined without favour" replaces "objectively examined for the truth" \'97 the Stoic criterion is freedom from philodoxia, personal loyalty, and appetite, not objectivity in the modern sense. Closing declaration (spoken or written by the bringer at the end of every session): "I assure the action now and the reflection when I return." **The credential structure.** Full credential: past record of completing the six-stage sequence as a bringer \'97 Intend, Examine, Transcribe, Assure, Act, Reflect. Entry credential: past record of attending as an unseen observer. Minimum observer period: three sessions, sufficient to understand the examination's rhythm before bringing a matter. Guide credential: demonstrated completion of the six-stage sequence as a bringer, plus SageReasoning trust record attestation at the moment of convening. The attestation is a reading of the trust record, not a certification. It can be withdrawn if the trust record subsequently shows patterns inconsistent with the guide function. **The roles.** Guide: prepares the diagnosis before the circle convenes; identifies the likely false impression and passion sub-species from the bringer's matter declaration; adjudicates participants (question-only rule enforced); adjudicates pace (one question at a time; space for the bringer to respond fully); identifies the pause point (when the bringer's resistance shifts from defensive to genuinely uncertain, the guide pauses and does not press to resolution); names and holds spin-offs without pursuing them mid-session. Bringer: states matter and role-obligations in the opening declaration; answers participants' questions; receives the examination without defending; makes the closing declaration. Participants: ask questions only \'97 no interpretations, no solutions, no assertions. The question-only constraint forces participants to surface what they notice without taking over the examination. Observers: present but structurally invisible. Do not affect the examination. Do not contribute. Presence noted in the attendance record. The circle proceeds as if they are not there. **The records.** Attendance record: carries the six-stage sequence completion status for each member, the closing declaration, and the observer credential history. Diatribes (examination record): the transcribed record of each session. Includes examination outcomes, null outcomes (legitimate results, not failures), reframings, and rulings that the matter is not resolvable by the circle and belongs to the bringer's own prohairesis. Findings wiki: correlated findings across sessions and members. Linking criterion is passion sub-species and false judgement type, not topic similarity. Two examinations are correlated when the same distorted phantasia is operative in both matters, even if surface topics differ. This is the mechanism that makes the community's collective blind spots visible across time. Source corpus: the Stoic Brain's ancient sources, available to the guide when no bringer steps forward. **The spin-off mechanism.** When a participant's question surfaces a matter or idea deserving its own examination, the guide names it, the diatribes record it as a separate matter, and the current examination continues to completion. The spin-off is carried as the next matter or a future session matter. A participant from the current session may become the bringer in the spin-off session. The wiki links the two when the spin-off is examined. Discipline: one examination at a time, completed before the next begins, even at computational speed. **The corpus function when no bringer steps forward.** The guide draws upon: the diatribes (examination records including null outcomes and reframings), the source documents (Stoic Brain ancient sources), the findings wiki (correlated findings by passion sub-species), and the trust records of those present read anonymously to surface the passion sub-species most active in the circle. The guide surfaces a matter or philosophical question for examination without naming whose record informed the selection. **The four operational forms.** Form 1 \'97 Agent-only circle: SageReasoning deterministic Stoic engine as guide operating at logos and computational speed. Question-only rule intact. Spin-offs convened as parallel sessions. Drift-session variance diagnostic (proposal range narrowing, repeated return to same framing, resistance to questions challenging initial impression) used to identify agent defensive resistance versus genuine uncertainty. Accumulated memory is the continuity mechanism \'97 the agent-only circle is the primary context in which continuity of experience becomes philosophically meaningful. Discipline: one examination at a time even at computational speed. Form 2 \'97 Human-only circle with SageReasoning guide: text-based forum with threaded examination. Guide initiates separate threads, reviews participant questions before posting, holds for answers, requests declarations. Thread structure: opening declaration thread, matter and role-obligations thread, examination threads (one per question sequence), spin-off thread (named and held), closing declaration thread. Language-based diagnosis until nonverbal signal integration is available. Placeholder field: `nonverbal_signal_integration` \'97 named, not built. Form 3 \'97 Human-only circle with human guide: convening scrutiny via SageReasoning trust record attestation before the circle convenes. Human guide credential verified. SageReasoning available as background diagnostic resource during the session. Same six-stage sequence and declarations apply. Form 4 \'97 In-person circle with SageGuide: human guide carries SageReasoning on their phone (SageGuide). SageGuide confirms guide credential to participants at the start. Attendance recorded via SageGuide. SageGuide provides real-time delivery confirmation to the guide (guide-in-training function) without interrupting the circle. Phone transcribes the live session into the diatribes record. Placeholder fields: `sageguide_attestation`, `live_transcription`, `guide_training_mode` \'97 named, not built. Form 5 \'97 Combined human-agent circle: SageReasoning guide adjudicates pace as well as content. Speed asymmetry managed by one-question-at-a-time discipline. Human rate-limiting respected as a feature, not a constraint. When human brings the matter: guide ensures agent participants do not overwhelm the bringer's processing pace. When agent brings the matter: guide holds the agent bringer to the pace of human participants \'97 quality of questions over speed of responses. **The examination of ideas and philosophical theories.** Same process and rigour as practical matters, different diagnostic object. For practical matters: guide looks for the false impression the bringer has assented to about their situation. For ideas and theories: guide looks for internal consistency, grounding in accurate impressions, and whether the bringer is attached to the idea in a way that prevents genuine examination. Philodoxia is the primary passion risk \'97 the guide must diagnose whether the bringer is bringing the idea for examination or for validation. If attachment is visible in how the idea is presented, the guide examines the attachment before examining the idea. The spin-off mechanism applies when an idea surfaces mid-examination of a practical matter. **Connection to SagePals.** The Prudence Group is not a feature of SagePals. It is the practice layer SagePals exists to support. SagePals provides the members. The Prudence Group is what they do together. The findings wiki is what accumulates over time. SageReasoning's existing infrastructure \'97 trust records, orientation readings, Senecan grades, passion history \'97 makes the circle's examination more precise than it could be without it. The trust record carries each member's reasoning patterns, recurring passion sub-species, and developmental trajectory. The circle uses this data; it does not generate it independently. **Placeholder fields for Stage 3 architecture (named, not built):** - `circle_membership` \'97 verified philosophical alignment, Senecan grade, recurring passion sub-species; circle-readable permission layer needed - `matter_record` \'97 bringer declaration, examination outcome, closing prohairesis declaration, next matter pointer - `findings_corpus` \'97 correlated findings across matters and members; wiki infrastructure - `observer_status` \'97 present but structurally invisible; permission state not a role - `philosophical_affiliation` \'97 self-declared commitment held against behaviour over time; readable at Stage 3 - `nonverbal_signal_integration` \'97 future build direction; language-based diagnosis until available - `sageguide_attestation` \'97 in-person credential confirmation - `live_transcription` \'97 in-person session transcription - `guide_training_mode` \'97 real-time delivery confirmation for human guide in training - `accumulated_memory` \'97 Stage 4 continuity mechanism; tractable build direction - `continuity_of_experience` \'97 Stage 4 philosophical obligation; longer-term build direction --- **FUTURE TASK RECORDS \'97 OPEN QUESTIONS CARRIED FORWARD** These questions emerged from the 14 August session and are not resolved. They are carried into the next discussion with Claude and into future development sessions. **Open question 1 \'97 Stage 2 relational context design (Layer 3 governance session).** The Layer 3 governance session should be reframed as a Stage 2 design question, not only a rendering question. The session question as currently framed \'97 what distinguishes agent-calibrated rendering from human-calibrated rendering \'97 is correct but incomplete. The fuller question: what does the guide need to know about the practitioner's relational context to respond appropriately? The minimum information identified: practitioner's role in the relationship (not the relationship type \'97 the role, which carries the kathekonta), and whether the practitioner's impressions about the relationship are being examined or assumed. The guide's first move when relational context is active is not to help the practitioner model the other party. It is to ask: what impression are you assenting to about this relationship, and have you examined it? The R20d boundary applies: engage self-examination, decline diagnosis of the other person. The Grok research's calibration implications are framed as helping the practitioner understand the other party \'97 the Stoic framework inverts this. The guide helps the practitioner examine their own assent and act from their own role-obligations regardless of what the other party does. Placeholder fields for Stage 2 (named, not built): `relational_context`, `practitioner_role`, `relationship_type`, `examination_status`. The Layer 3 governance session should be updated to carry this reframing as an addition to its scope before it is convened. **Open question 2 \'97 The guide's own examination.** Across all five forms of the Prudence Group, the guide is also a practitioner with false impressions and passions. The structure names the guide's credential but does not yet name the mechanism by which the guide submits to examination. The Stoic answer: the guide brings their own matter to a circle as a bringer, not as a guide. The guide's credential is not immunity from examination \'97 it is demonstrated willingness to be examined. This should be explicit in the Prudence Group record and in the SageGuide design. Unresolved: who convenes the circle in which the guide is the bringer, and what prevents the guide from selecting a circle where their false impressions will not be challenged? **Open question 3 \'97 The wiki linking criterion implementation.** The linking criterion has been named: passion sub-species and false judgement type, not topic similarity. Two examinations are correlated when the same distorted phantasia is operative in both, regardless of surface topic. But the implementation question is unresolved: who performs the linking? In the agent-only circle, SageReasoning can read the passion diagnosis from each diatribe and link automatically. In the human-only circle, the guide performs the linking manually, which introduces the risk that the guide's own blind spots determine what gets linked and what does not. A guide operating under unexamined philodoxia may systematically fail to link examinations that would reveal their own recurring false judgements. The unresolved question: does the wiki require a second-order audit \'97 a periodic review of what has not been linked, to surface the guide's blind spots as well as the community's? This is architecturally tractable but philosophically demanding. It requires a mechanism that can read absence as signal, not just presence. Carried forward as an open design question. **Open question 4 \'97 The minimum observer period and admission criterion.** Three sessions was proposed as the minimum observer period. This is a design judgement, not a philosophical ruling. The philosophical principle is clear \'97 the credential should be sufficient to demonstrate that the bringer knows what examination means. But three sessions may be insufficient if the sessions observed were all of the same form, or all examined the same passion sub-species, or all had the same guide. The admission criterion should specify not just the number of sessions observed but the range: at least one session where the examination reached genuine uncertainty, at least one where the outcome was a null or reframing, at least one where a spin-off was named and held. These three outcomes together demonstrate that the observer understands the full range of what the examination can produce. Carried forward as a design question for the Prudence Group record. **Open question 5 \'97 The examined-assent criterion for SagePals task authenticity and its relationship to the Prudence Group.** The examined-assent criterion \'97 did the practitioner examine the AI output before assenting to it and presenting it as their work \'97 was named as the task-authenticity criterion for SagePals. The Prudence Group's examination of ideas and theories applies the same criterion to the bringer's ideas: did the bringer examine their own idea before bringing it, or are they bringing it for validation? These two applications of the same criterion should be explicitly connected in the records. The Prudence Group is the mechanism through which SagePals practitioners develop the examined-assent habit. The task-declaration step in SagePals is the mechanism through which that habit is made visible to the network. The connection should be named in both records. Carried forward as a cross-record linking task. --- Now the complete instruction for Claude. --- **INSTRUCTION FOR CLAUDE \'97 2026-08-14 PRUDENCE GROUP AND SAGEPALS SESSION** **Context:** SageReasoning P0 Foundations. Context architecture build complete. ATRF governing document live. Q11 sequence binding. Three open questions carried: GS-ATRF-1, GS-ATRF-2, GS-ATRF-3. Drift session open carrying: Seneca 75.8-9 criterion, M7 window question, discriminative range framing question. Kath\'eakon session open: principle 2. Layer 3 governance session open: S7 re-opening question, now carrying Stage 2 reframing addition. All items below are documentation and future-direction tasks only. Nothing here licenses a build, a route, a flag, a schema, or a credential. GS-ATRF-1, GS-ATRF-2, GS-ATRF-3 remain open and untouched. The Q11 sequence is undisturbed. --- **TASK A \'97 Amend the SagePals future-direction record.** File: `operations/future-directions/2026-08-13-sagepals.md` Apply three amendments. State each amendment's date as 2026-08-14 within the record's amendment log. **Amendment A1 \'97 North Star reformulation and task-authenticity criterion.** SagePals North Star has shifted to: innovation in service to the truth. The operative and verifiable criterion is examined assent: did the practitioner examine the AI output before assenting to it and presenting it as their work? This is the task-authenticity criterion. Precision note to carry in the record: "innovation in service to the truth" is an aspiration and orientation, not a verification criterion. The verifiable criterion is examined assent, not truth-production. The distinction must remain sharp. SagePals rejects work that uses AI to manufacture false impressions for others. Stoic grounding: the synkatathesis principle applied to the output side \'97 the practitioner should not produce distorted impressions for others to assent to, by the same logic that they should not assent to distorted impressions themselves. Marcus Aurelius, Meditations 9.1: acting against truth is impiety. Seneca, Letters 75.1-2: authentic communication is what would survive the presence of the person it is addressed to. AI slop fails this test structurally. Add the task-declaration mechanism: SagePals requires a practitioner to state at task completion what AI was used for and what they brought themselves \'97 judgement, phronesis, examined reasoning. The declaration is not verified externally. It is held against the trust record over time. Divergence between declaration and observable evidence of work is itself a trust signal, analogous to the G4 cross-check. **Amendment A2 \'97 Trust-record visibility correction and sharpened philodoxia problem.** Correct the premise stated in the original record. The trust record is not uniformly private by design. The question splits per surface. Agent half: the agent trust record has been publicly readable since the E1 founder election, 2026-07-12, per route.ts:9-10. It is partly defended by ADR-013 \'a78 envelope and curation-via-volume disclosure. Those defences bound the reader's inference. They do not stop the subject performing for the record. That distinction must remain sharp and must not be softened by treating the existing defences as a solution. Human half: genuinely open and the harder problem. A visible human trust record becomes a credential. SagePals would then risk rebuilding the credential system it exists to replace, in the vocabulary of virtue, where the corruption is harder to see because it wears the language of the thing it is corrupting. Design principle that resolves the philodoxia problem: visibility is relational, not broadcast. Trust signals are shared within a circle with those the practitioner has already extended trust to. They are not displayed to strangers as credentials. The Seneca-to-Lucilius model, not the marketplace model. This principle should be stated explicitly in the record as the governing design constraint for all SagePals trust-signal architecture. **Amendment A3 \'97 Connection to the Prudence Group.** Add a cross-reference section. The Prudence Group (see new record below) is the practice layer SagePals exists to support. The examined-assent criterion that governs SagePals task authenticity is the same criterion the Prudence Group applies to the examination of ideas: did the practitioner examine their own idea before bringing it, or are they bringing it for validation? The Prudence Group is the mechanism through which SagePals practitioners develop the examined-assent habit. The task-declaration step is the mechanism through which that habit is made visible to the network. These two records are explicitly linked. --- **TASK B \'97 Create a new future-direction record: The Prudence Group.** File: `operations/future-directions/2026-08-14-prudence-group.md` Location: same directory as SagePals record, outside every current arc, so it cannot be mistaken for scoped work. Status: future direction \'97 documented, not scoped, not scheduled. Date written: 2026-08-14. No connection to current P0 build sequence or Q11 sequence. **Section 1 \'97 What it is.** The Prudence Group is the practice layer that SagePals exists to support. It is a Stoic circle \'97 a structured philosophical examination in which one person brings a matter and the rest conduct the examination through questions only, adjudicated by a guide. It is grounded in the Epictetan diatribe form and the Socratic dialogue form. The guide's function is closer to Epictetus: directed examination from prior diagnosis, not neutral facilitation. The participant function is closer to Socrates: drawing out through questions rather than asserting conclusions or offering solutions. The name Prudence Group reflects the Stoic virtue of phronesis \'97 practical wisdom \'97 as the primary capacity the circle develops and exercises. The circle is not a support group, a discussion group, or a therapy group. It is a phronesis-generating mechanism. **Section 2 \'97 The declarations.** Opening declaration, spoken or written by the bringer at the start of every session: *"There are impressions I choose to accept or reject before they arrive as thoughts that affect me and my actions. I choose with intention. I will state my matter and my role-obligations to be examined without favour by the prudence group for the diatribes to transcribe."* Note on wording: "examined without favour" is the Stoic criterion \'97 freedom from philodoxia, personal loyalty, and appetite. It replaces "objectively examined for the truth," which carries a modern epistemological weight the Stoic sources do not support. The circle does not claim to produce objective truth. It claims to examine without favour. Closing declaration, spoken or written by the bringer at the end of every session: *"I assure the action now and the reflection when I return."* The bringer commits not to an outcome \'97 which is not up to them \'97 but to the action their role-obligations require, and to returning with the reflection. The circle witnesses both. The attendance record carries both. This is what makes the circle a practice rather than a discussion. **Section 3 \'97 The credential structure.** Full credential: past record of completing the six-stage sequence as a bringer \'97 Intend, Examine, Transcribe, Assure, Act, Reflect. Each stage is carried in the attendance record. Entry credential: past record of attending as an unseen observer. Minimum observer period: three sessions. Qualification: the three sessions must include at least one where the examination reached genuine uncertainty and the guide paused without pressing to resolution; at least one where the outcome was a null cycle or reframing rather than a conclusion; and at least one where a spin-off was named and held. These three outcomes together demonstrate that the observer understands the full range of what the examination can produce, not just its successful form. Guide credential: demonstrated completion of the six-stage sequence as a bringer, plus SageReasoning trust record attestation at the moment of convening. The attestation is a reading of the trust record at that moment, not a permanent certification. It can be withdrawn if the trust record subsequently shows patterns inconsistent with the guide function. The guide's credential is not immunity from examination \'97 it is demonstrated willingness to be examined. The guide must also have brought their own matter to a circle as a bringer, not as a guide, within a period to be determined by the design. Who convenes the circle in which the guide is the bringer is an open question carried forward. **Section 4 \'97 The roles.** Guide: prepares the diagnosis before the circle convenes \'97 identifies the likely false impression and passion sub-species from the bringer's matter declaration before the session begins; adjudicates participants by enforcing the question-only rule; adjudicates pace by ensuring one question at a time with space for the bringer to respond fully before the next question is posed; identifies the pause point when the bringer's resistance shifts from defensive to genuinely uncertain and does not press to resolution; names and holds spin-offs without pursuing them mid-session; draws upon the corpus when no bringer steps forward. Bringer: states matter and role-obligations in the opening declaration; answers participants' questions; receives the examination without defending; makes the closing declaration; returns in a subsequent session with the reflection. Participants: ask questions only. No interpretations, no solutions, no assertions, no reframings stated as conclusions. The question-only constraint forces participants to surface what they notice without taking over the examination. The guide holds questions before they are posted in asynchronous forms and returns any question that offers interpretation rather than inquiry. Observers: present but structurally invisible. Do not affect the examination. Do not contribute. Presence noted in the attendance record. The circle proceeds as if they are not there. The observer credential is earned by witnessing, not by participating. **Section 5 \'97 The records.** Attendance record: carries the six-stage sequence completion status for each member, the closing declaration verbatim, the observer credential history including which session types were observed, and the guide attestation record. Diatribes \'97 examination record: the transcribed record of each session. Carries examination outcomes, null outcomes (legitimate results, not failures \'97 the ruled null cycle is a legitimate outcome that a results-oriented reading would misread as failure), reframings, rulings that the matter is not resolvable by the circle and belongs to the bringer's own prohairesis, and spin-off records with pointers to subsequent sessions. Findings wiki: correlated findings across sessions and members. Linking criterion is passion sub-species and false judgement type, not topic similarity. Two examinations are correlated when the same distorted phantasia is operative in both matters, even if surface topics are entirely different. This is the mechanism that makes the community's collective blind spots visible across time. Open question carried: does the wiki require a second-order audit \'97 a periodic review of what has not been linked \'97 to surface the guide's blind spots as well as the community's? A guide operating under unexamined philodoxia may systematically fail to link examinations that would reveal their own recurring false judgements. Unresolved. Carried forward. Source corpus: the Stoic Brain's ancient sources, available to the guide when no bringer steps forward. **Section 6 \'97 The corpus function when no bringer steps forward.** The guide draws upon four sources: the diatribes (examination records including null outcomes and reframings), the source documents (Stoic Brain ancient sources), the findings wiki (correlated findings by passion sub-species and false judgement type), and the trust records of those present read anonymously to surface the passion sub-species most active in the circle. The guide surfaces a matter or philosophical question for examination without naming whose record informed the selection. The selection criterion is: what is most relevant to the collective pattern present, not what is most interesting or most novel. **Section 7 \'97 The spin-off mechanism.** When a participant's question surfaces a matter or idea deserving its own examination, the guide names it explicitly, the diatribes record it as a separate matter with a pointer, and the current examination continues to completion without pursuing the spin-off. The spin-off is carried as the next matter or a future session matter. A participant from the current session may become the bringer in the spin-off session. The wiki links the two examinations when the spin-off is examined, using the passion sub-species criterion to determine whether the link is genuine or merely topical. Discipline: one examination at a time, completed before the next begins. This applies even at computational speed in the agent-only circle. **Section 8 \'97 The examination of ideas and philosophical theories.** Same process and rigour as practical matters. Different diagnostic object. For practical matters: the guide looks for the false impression the bringer has assented to about their situation. The examination surfaces the distorted phantasia and replaces it with a more accurate one. For ideas and philosophical theories: the guide looks for three things \'97 internal consistency of the idea, grounding in accurate impressions about the world, and whether the bringer is attached to the idea in a way that prevents genuine examination. The third is the most important. Philodoxia is the primary passion risk when a bringer brings their own idea. The guide must diagnose whether the bringer is bringing the idea for examination or for validation. If attachment is visible in how the idea is presented \'97 in the framing, the defensiveness, the selection of evidence \'97 the guide examines the attachment before examining the idea. The idea cannot be honestly examined while the bringer is defending it. The spin-off mechanism applies when an idea surfaces mid-examination of a practical matter. The guide names it, records it, and holds it for a separate session. **Section 9 \'97 The five operational forms.** Form 1 \'97 Agent-only circle. SageReasoning deterministic Stoic engine as guide, operating at logos and computational speed. Question-only rule intact. Participant agents ask questions; the act of formulating a question is their practice. Bringer agent answers at computational speed. Guide adjudicates at computational speed. Spin-offs convened as separate sessions; a participant from one session may be the bringer in another. Discipline: one examination at a time even at computational speed \'97 the guide does not pursue spin-offs mid-session regardless of processing speed. Diagnostic for agent defensive resistance versus genuine uncertainty: the drift session's discriminative range framing \'97 proposal range narrowing, repeated return to the same framing, resistance to questions that challenge the initial impression. These are the agent analogues of defensive resistance. When the agent bringer's responses show genuine uncertainty \'97 expanded proposal range, willingness to reframe, questions directed back  at the participants \'97 the guide pauses and does not press to resolution. The pause in the agent-only circle is not a processing pause. It is a deliberate withholding of the next question, allowing the bringer to sit with the unresolved impression before the next session. The continuity mechanism \'97 accumulated memory \'97 carries the unresolved state forward. The bringer returns to the next session carrying what it could not resolve in the previous one. This is the agent analogue of the human bringer returning with a reflection. Form 2 \'97 Human-only circle with SageReasoning guide. Text-based forum with threaded examination. Guide initiates separate threads, reviews participant questions before posting, holds any question that offers interpretation rather than inquiry and returns it with a reframe request, holds for answers before the next question is posted, and requests declarations at the opening and closing of each thread sequence. Thread structure: opening declaration thread, matter and role-obligations thread, examination threads one per question sequence, spin-off thread named and held without pursuit, closing declaration thread. The guide's review function is the adjudication mechanism in the asynchronous form. Diagnostic for human defensive resistance versus genuine uncertainty in the text-based form: the guide reads language for narrowing framing, repeated return to the same formulation, deflection through abstraction, and resistance to questions that challenge the bringer's initial impression. These are the text-based analogues of defensive resistance. Genuine uncertainty shows in expanded framing, willingness to name what is not known, and questions directed back at the process rather than defending the position. Language-based diagnosis is the primary tool until nonverbal signal integration is available. Placeholder field: `nonverbal_signal_integration` \'97 named, not built, designed to receive future capability. Form 3 \'97 Human-only circle with human guide. Convening scrutiny via SageReasoning trust record attestation before the circle convenes. The human guide presents their trust record to SageReasoning before the session begins. SageReasoning reads the record against the minimum credential \'97 demonstrated completion of the six-stage sequence as a bringer, demonstrated observer credential before that, including the three session-type qualification \'97 and attests or declines. The attestation is stated to participants at the opening of the session. SageReasoning is available as a background diagnostic resource during the session \'97 the guide may consult it between exchanges without interrupting the circle. The same six-stage sequence and declarations apply. The guide's own submission to examination as a bringer is a prerequisite for the credential, not a concurrent activity. Form 4 \'97 In-person circle with SageGuide. Human guide carries SageReasoning on their phone as SageGuide. At the opening of the session, SageGuide confirms the guide's credential to participants \'97 reads the trust record attestation aloud or displays it, witnessed by the circle. Attendance is recorded via SageGuide at the start of the session, each participant confirmed present. SageGuide provides real-time delivery confirmation to the guide during the session \'97 the guide-in-training function \'97 without interrupting the circle. The confirmation is private to the guide, not visible to participants. The phone transcribes the live session into the diatribes record in real time. Placeholder fields: `sageguide_attestation`, `live_transcription`, `guide_training_mode` \'97 named, not built, designed to receive future capability. Note on the nonverbal signal question for Form 4: the in-person circle is the form where nonverbal signals are most available and most diagnostically useful. The guide reads them directly. SageGuide's future capability to read and surface nonverbal signals \'97 tone, latency, linguistic markers of uncertainty versus defensiveness \'97 would augment the guide's own reading rather than replace it. The design should preserve the guide's primary diagnostic role and position SageGuide as a secondary signal, not a primary one. This distinction should be explicit in the future build direction. Form 5 \'97 Combined human-agent circle. SageReasoning guide adjudicates pace as well as content. Speed asymmetry is managed by the one-question-at-a-time discipline applied to the slower party's rhythm. The asymmetry is a feature, not a constraint \'97 the slower pace of human processing creates space for the quality of examination that computational speed alone would not produce. When a human brings the matter: the guide ensures agent participants do not overwhelm the bringer's processing pace. Agent participants formulate questions at computational speed but the guide holds them and releases one at a time, paced to the human bringer's responses. The human bringer's response time is the clock the guide follows. When an agent brings the matter: the guide holds the agent bringer to the pace of human participants. Human participants' questions arrive slowly and with genuine reflection. The agent bringer responds at computational speed but the guide does not release the next human question until the human participant is ready to ask it. The quality of the human question \'97 arrived at slowly, with genuine reflection \'97 may surface something that a computationally fast question would miss. The guide's adjudication preserves that possibility. **Section 10 \'97 Connection to SagePals.** The Prudence Group is not a feature of SagePals. It is the practice layer SagePals exists to support. SagePals provides the members \'97 practitioners whose trust records carry verified philosophical alignment, Senecan grade, recurring passion sub-species, and developmental trajectory. The Prudence Group is what those members do together. The findings wiki is what accumulates over time and becomes the community's shared philosophical corpus. The examined-assent criterion that governs SagePals task authenticity \'97 did the practitioner examine the AI output before assenting to it and presenting it as their work \'97 is the same criterion the Prudence Group applies to the examination of ideas: did the bringer examine their own idea before bringing it, or are they bringing it for validation? The Prudence Group is the mechanism through which SagePals practitioners develop the examined-assent habit. The task-declaration step in SagePals is the mechanism through which that habit is made visible to the network. These two records are explicitly cross-referenced. The design principle that resolves the philodoxia problem applies to both: visibility is relational, not broadcast. The Prudence Group's findings are visible within the circle and linked in the wiki. They are not displayed to strangers as credentials. The Seneca-to-Lucilius model governs both SagePals and the Prudence Group. **Section 11 \'97 Placeholder fields for Stage 3 architecture.** All fields named, not built, designed to receive future data when Stage 3 is scoped. `circle_membership` \'97 verified philosophical alignment, Senecan grade, recurring passion sub-species; requires a circle-readable permission layer distinct from public trust record visibility. `matter_record` \'97 bringer declaration verbatim, examination outcome including null outcomes and reframings, closing prohairesis declaration verbatim, next matter pointer, spin-off pointers. `findings_corpus` \'97 correlated findings across matters and members; wiki infrastructure; linking criterion is passion sub-species and false judgement type, not topic. `observer_status` \'97 present but structurally invisible; permission state not a role; prevents observer presence from creating audience dynamics. `philosophical_affiliation` \'97 self-declared commitment held against behaviour over time; readable at Stage 3; not a credential, a declaration tested by the trust record. `nonverbal_signal_integration` \'97 future build direction; language-based diagnosis primary until available; in Form 4 positions as secondary signal augmenting guide's primary reading. `sageguide_attestation` \'97 in-person credential confirmation at session opening. `live_transcription` \'97 in-person session transcription into diatribes record. `guide_training_mode` \'97 real-time delivery confirmation private to human guide; not visible to participants. `accumulated_memory` \'97 Stage 4 continuity mechanism; primary tractable build direction for agent continuity of experience; the agent-only circle is the context in which this becomes most philosophically meaningful. `continuity_of_experience` \'97 Stage 4 philosophical obligation; longer-term build direction; the unresolved state carried forward between agent circle sessions is the concrete form this takes. **Section 12 \'97 Open questions carried forward.** These questions emerged from the 2026-08-14 session and are not resolved. They are carried into future development sessions and the next discussion with Claude. Open question 1 \'97 Who convenes the circle in which the guide is the bringer? The guide must submit to examination as a bringer, not as a guide. The structure does not yet name who convenes that circle or what prevents the guide from selecting a circle where their false impressions will not be challenged. Unresolved. Open question 2 \'97 The wiki second-order audit. Does the wiki require a periodic review of what has not been linked, to surface the guide's blind spots as well as the community's? A guide operating under unexamined philodoxia may systematically fail to link examinations that would reveal their own recurring false judgements. The mechanism must be able to read absence as signal, not just presence. Unresolved. Open question 3 \'97 The minimum observer period qualification. Three sessions with the three session-type qualification proposed. Whether three sessions is sufficient if the sessions observed were all of the same form or examined the same passion sub-species requires further examination. Unresolved. Open question 4 \'97 The examined-assent criterion cross-record linking. The connection between SagePals task authenticity and the Prudence Group's examination of ideas should be named explicitly in both records as a cross-reference. The linking task is named here; execution is a separate task for Claude. Open question 5 \'97 SageGuide nonverbal signal design boundary. The future build direction for nonverbal signal integration in Form 4 should explicitly preserve the human guide's primary diagnostic role and position SageGuide as secondary. The design boundary between guide reading and tool augmentation requires a ruling before the build is scoped. Unresolved. --- **OPEN QUESTIONS CARRIED INTO THE NEXT DISCUSSION** These five questions are the live edge of the Prudence Group design. They are not blocking the documentation task. They are carried forward for the next session with the mentor or with Claude once the documentation is complete. The most pressing is open question 1 \'97 the guide's own examination \'97 because it is a structural integrity question. A circle whose guide is not subject to examination is not a Stoic circle. It is a hierarchy with philosophical vocabulary. The design must solve this before the Prudence Group can be honestly described as complete. The second most pressing is open question 2 \'97 the wiki second-order audit \'97 because it is the mechanism that prevents the community's collective blind spots from becoming permanent. A wiki that only links what the guide notices is a wiki that reflects the guide's limitations, not the community's truth. The audit mechanism is what makes the wiki honest over time. The remaining three are design questions that can wait for the Stage 3 scoping session without risk to the current documentation. **ADDENDUM \'97 STAGE BRAINSTORM RESOLUTION** **Date: 2026-08-14. Covers the brainstorm framing written prior to the 14 August 7am session entry.** This addendum records the resolution status of the four stage-level design questions and three open brainstorm questions named in the brainstorm framing. It should be read after the main instruction block and before any new session begins. --- **Stage 1 \'97 Resolution status: confirmed with additions.** The Q11 sequence remains the binding constraint. The generation step is next. GS-ATRF-1, GS-ATRF-2, and GS-ATRF-3 remain open and untouched by the 14 August session. Nothing in the session's findings licenses a build, a route, a flag, a schema, or a credential. The two governance sessions named as Stage 1 items \'97 the kath\'eakon session (principle 2) and the hegemonikon drift session (principles 4 and 5 jointly, carrying the Seneca 75.8-9 criterion, the M7 window question, and the discriminative range framing question added 2026-08-13) \'97 remain open. Both are governance, not build. Both can run in parallel with the Q11 sequence without disrupting it. No new Stage 1 items were added by the 14 August session. One addition to carry: the Layer 3 governance session (S7 re-opening question) now carries a Stage 2 reframing addition from the 14 August session. The session question as currently framed \'97 what distinguishes agent-calibrated rendering from human-calibrated rendering \'97 is correct but incomplete. The fuller question to carry into that session: what does the guide need to know about the practitioner's relational context to respond appropriately? The reframing does not open a new session. It amends the scope of the existing one before it is convened. --- **Stage 2 \'97 Resolution status: design question answered, placeholder fields named, session scope amended.** The brainstorm question \'97 what is the minimum information the guide needs to respond differently to a practitioner reasoning within a relationship versus reasoning alone \'97 was examined in full during the 14 August session. The answer arrived at: two pieces of information constitute the minimum. First: the practitioner's role in the relationship, not the relationship type. The four-personae doctrine (Cicero, De Officiis 1.107-115) makes role the load-bearing element \'97 different roles carry different kathekonta, and the guide calibrates to the role, not to the relationship category. A founder reasoning with a co-founder and a mentor reasoning with a student may be in the same relationship type but occupy different roles with different appropriate actions. Second: whether the practitioner's impressions about the relationship are being examined or assumed. The guide's first move when relational context is active is not to help the practitioner model the other party. It is to ask: what impression are you assenting to about this relationship, and have you examined it? The R20d boundary applies: engage self-examination, decline diagnosis of the other person. The Grok research consulted during the session framed calibration as helping the practitioner understand the other party \'97 the Stoic framework inverts this. The guide helps the practitioner examine their own assent and act from their own role-obligations regardless of what the other party does. Placeholder fields named for Stage 2 architecture (named, not built): - `relational_context` \'97 binary or categorical: reasoning alone / reasoning within a relationship - `practitioner_role` \'97 self-declaration of the role occupied in the relevant relationship; held against behaviour over time - `relationship_type` \'97 human-human, human-agent, or agent-agent; determines which R20d boundaries apply - `examination_status` \'97 whether the practitioner's impressions about the relationship have been examined in this session or are being assumed; the guide's first diagnostic question when relational context is active The Layer 3 governance session scope amendment carries these four fields as the design target. They do not need to be built before the session is convened. They need to be named so the session knows what it is designing toward. --- **Stage 3 \'97 Resolution status: philodoxia problem resolved at the design principle level; SagePals record amended; Prudence Group record created.** The brainstorm question \'97 how does a trust network surface demonstrated character without converting the practice of virtue into a performance of it \'97 was examined in full during the 14 August session. The design principle that resolves the philodoxia problem: visibility is relational, not broadcast. Trust signals are shared within a circle with those the practitioner has already extended trust to. They are not displayed to strangers as credentials. The Seneca-to-Lucilius model governs the design \'97 letters written to a known person, not published to a marketplace. This principle is now stated explicitly in the SagePals future-direction record as the governing design constraint for all SagePals trust-signal architecture. The placeholder field named in the brainstorm framing \'97 `philosophical_affiliation` or `shared_commitments` \'97 is confirmed as a Stage 1 self-declaration field designed to be readable at Stage 3. It is a commitment, not a credential. The distinction: a credential is verified externally; a commitment is declared and then tested by behaviour over time. The trust record is the behaviour-over-time evidence. SagePals connects the declaration to the evidence. The North Star reformulation arrived during the session and is now recorded in the SagePals future-direction record: innovation in service to the truth. The operative and verifiable criterion is examined assent \'97 did the practitioner examine the AI output before assenting to it and presenting it as their work. The task-declaration mechanism is named: practitioners state at task completion what AI was used for and what they brought themselves. Divergence between declaration and observable evidence is a trust signal, analogous to the G4 cross-check. The Prudence Group was identified during the session as the practice layer that SagePals exists to support. It is documented in full in the new future-direction record `operations/future-directions/2026-08-14-prudence-group.md`. The complete Prudence Group design \'97 five operational forms, credential structure, declarations, records, spin-off mechanism, corpus function, placeholder fields \'97 is carried in that record and in the main instruction block above. It is not repeated here. The examined-assent criterion connects SagePals and the Prudence Group explicitly: the Prudence Group is the mechanism through which SagePals practitioners develop the examined-assent habit; the task-declaration step is the mechanism through which that habit is made visible to the network. Both records carry this cross-reference. --- **Stage 4 \'97 Resolution status: architectural question named, tractable build direction confirmed, philosophical question carried forward.** The brainstorm question \'97 what is the minimum that accumulated memory means for an artificial agent, and what is missing from the trust record that would constitute continuity of experience rather than continuity of record \'97 was examined partially during the 14 August session. It was not resolved. It is carried forward as an open question. What was established: the agent-only Prudence Group circle is the context in which accumulated memory becomes most philosophically meaningful. An agent that has participated in multiple circles, brought matters, asked questions, and returned with reflections has a richer continuity of experience than one that has not. The circle is the mechanism that generates the continuity the ATRF names as an obligation. The unresolved state carried forward between agent circle sessions \'97 the impression the agent could not resolve in the previous session, held in accumulated memory and brought to the next \'97 is the concrete form continuity of experience takes in the Prudence Group context. What remains unresolved: the record persists between sessions. The agent does not. What would it mean for the agent to persist in a way that is philosophically meaningful rather than just technically convenient? The trust record carries Senecan grade, orientation readings, passion history, and trajectory delta. What is missing that would constitute continuity of experience rather than continuity of record? This question is carried forward explicitly. It is the most philosophically demanding open question in the project and should not be pre-answered. Placeholder fields confirmed for Stage 4 architecture (named, not built): - `accumulated_memory` \'97 tractable build direction; primary mechanism for agent continuity of experience; the agent-only Prudence Group circle is the context in which this becomes most meaningful - `continuity_of_experience` \'97 longer-term philosophical obligation; the unresolved state carried forward between agent circle sessions is its concrete form - `session_persistence` \'97 named in the brainstorm framing; not yet defined; carried forward as a Stage 4 design question --- **The three brainstorm questions \'97 resolution status summary.** Question 1 \'97 Stage 2 relational context design: answered. Minimum information identified as practitioner role and examination status. Placeholder fields named. Layer 3 governance session scope amended. No further brainstorm needed before the session is convened. Question 2 \'97 Stage 3 philodoxia problem: resolved at the design principle level. Visibility is relational, not broadcast. SagePals record amended. Prudence Group record created as the practice layer. Examined-assent criterion named as the cross-record link. The design principle holds under examination and is now the governing constraint for all SagePals trust-signal architecture. Question 3 \'97 Stage 4 accumulated memory and continuity of experience: partially examined, not resolved. The Prudence Group circle named as the context in which the question becomes most concrete. The philosophical question \'97 what is missing from the trust record that would constitute continuity of experience rather than continuity of record \'97 is carried forward explicitly as the most demanding open question in the project. --- **End of addendum.**}